From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] winex11: a new window surface starts unshaped, and so does its X window

A window's surface goes when it is hidden, but its X window kept the shape mask
the old surface gave it; the new surface, with no shape of its own yet, never
reported a shape change at its first flush, so the old mask stayed. In the
Rounded style a flyout asking for round corners (the calendar, volume,
network) has a rounded shape (0492); after a switch to Classic, resized while
hidden and shown again, it showed only its old-size rounded area (cal1). With
client-side graphics the shape is the surface's: a new surface clears the X
window's mask, and a surface with a shape sets it again at its first flush.

Gate: make test-roundswitch. Mutant: -DSG_MUTANT_SURFACE_KEEPS_OLD_SHAPE.
---
--- a/dlls/winex11.drv/bitblt.c
+++ b/dlls/winex11.drv/bitblt.c
@@ -2298,6 +2298,16 @@
         surface->window = window;
         surface->gc = XCreateGC( gdi_display, window, 0, NULL );
         XSetSubwindowMode( gdi_display, surface->gc, IncludeInferiors );
+#if defined(HAVE_LIBXSHAPE) && !defined(SG_MUTANT_SURFACE_KEEPS_OLD_SHAPE)
+        /* Stained Glass OS: a new surface starts unshaped, and so does its X window. A
+         * window's surface goes when it is hidden, but the X window keeps the shape mask
+         * the old surface gave it, and the new surface (no shape of its own yet) never
+         * says the shape changed: a flyout rounded in the Rounded style, resized while
+         * hidden after a switch to Classic, came back showing only its old rounded
+         * area (cal1). A surface with a shape sets it again at its first flush. */
+        if (client_side_graphics)
+            XShapeCombineMask( gdi_display, window, ShapeBounding, 0, 0, None, ShapeSet );
+#endif
     }
 
     return window_surface;
